# libbox64dl patch 0020: engine-side signal action integration.
#
# The application signal action module (include/libbox64_signal_actions.h,
# src/signal_actions.c) owns SIGSEGV/SIGBUS/SIGILL/SIGABRT from init on and
# decides DEFAULT / IGNORE / NATIVE / GUEST for every delivery. This patch
# grafts the two engine-side pieces that module needs and nothing else.
#
# src/libtools/signals.c
#   * defines box64dl_engine_signal_hook, the pointer the module declares, as
#     the engine's 4-argument repair handler. my_box64signalhandler() is kept
#     as a thin compatibility wrapper that calls the same body with the
#     "current variant" sentinel, so the MY_SIGHANDLER aliases keep linking.
#   * the handler body now PEEKS the emu (thread_peek_emu, never fabricating
#     one). A thread with no guest context cannot legitimately enter the
#     repairs, so such a fault is forwarded straight to box64dl_signal_deliver
#     with emu == NULL and the captured kernel variant.
#   * patches/0003's first-line call to box64dl_signal_route() is removed, and
#     with it the GS/TEB discrimination that misrouted plain Linux-x64 guests
#     (TLS in FS, GS base 0) to the host action.
#   * every genuine hardware repair stays FIRST: the unaligned-BUS fixup, the
#     ARCH_NOP callret recovery, the FillBlock abort, the PROT_DYNAREC SMC
#     unprotect with its auto-SMC resume, the PROT_DYNAREC_R unprotect, and the
#     cleanDBFromAddressRange/refreshProtection "multi process glitch" retries.
#     None of them consumes an application one-shot.
#   * only after all of them - and OUTSIDE #ifdef DYNAREC, so a build without
#     the dynarec still reaches the module - box64dl_signal_deliver() takes the
#     application decision WHILE the dynarec mutexes stay released (unlockMutex
#     ran above): an application callback must not inherit the engine locks, and
#     a foreign longjmp must not strand them. relockMutex() runs only on the
#     module's normal return, matching every other path through the handler.
#   * the diagnostics -- name/backtrace lookups, jitgdb, the register dump --
#     and the legacy guest-emulation body stay BELOW the decision. The
#     SEGV_ACCERR repeated-fault diagnostic is moved inside its own always-returns
#     branch, so no message is printed on a delivery that reaches the decision.
#     They are reached only from the compatibility wrapper or when the module is
#     inactive, and can never precede a native or guest user handler.
#   * a delivery with no siginfo, or si_code <= 0, goes straight to
#     box64dl_signal_deliver() with the kernel entry variant forwarded; a
#     hardware fault on a thread without a guest context goes the same way with
#     emu == NULL. Neither fabricates an emu.
#   * init_signal_helper() publishes the seam and then calls
#     box64dl_signal_actions_init(), which captures the pre-engine actions and
#     installs the module's four kernel entries transactionally; no legacy
#     kernel handler is installed afterwards, and a failed init leaves the
#     module inactive with the seam withdrawn. It also publishes the optional
#     NATIVE-invocation seam (box64dl_native_call_hook ->
#     box64dl_signal_guest_native_call) before any delivery can occur.
#   * fini_signal_helper() withdraws both seams, calls
#     box64dl_signal_actions_fini() (which restores only dispositions that are
#     still its own) and then box64dl_signal_guest_reset(). This replaces
#     patches/0006's per-signal restore.
#
# src/box64context.c
#   * fini_signal_helper() is moved out of finiAllHelpers() into
#     FreeBox64Context(), right after the forked guard. finiAllHelpers() runs
#     after ctx->system and the ELFs have been destroyed; the signal teardown
#     needs them alive.
#   * the raw `signal(i, SIG_DFL)` loop over ctx->signals[] is removed: every
#     tracked action now belongs to the module, and that loop would clobber
#     host actions the module owns.
#
# Vendor-only change, kept here so vendor/box64 stays re-syncable; the Makefile
# patch target applies it.

